(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What decrease in airflow, measured by thermistor, defines an apnea?","choices":["At least 30%","At least 50%","At least 75%","At least 90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An apnea is a decrease in airflow of 90% or more, measured by thermistor and lasting at least 10 seconds: an episode of near or total breathing cessation. Breathing that is slowed, shallow and restricted in 10-second or longer episodes is called a hypopnea instead. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which history combination best fits obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Insomnia, nocturnal dyspnea, little snoring","Exertional dyspnea, pedal edema, facial puffiness","Refreshing sleep and quiet nights","Morning headaches, nocturia, low libido"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Morning headaches, nocturia and decreased libido are history findings of obstructive sleep apnea, together with daytime sleepiness, unrefreshing sleep, witnessed apnea, loud habitual snoring, poorly controlled hypertension and nocturnal sweating. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 25, 31"},{"q":"Among the sex-specific risk factors for obstructive sleep apnea, what neck circumference is the threshold for men?","choices":["15 inches","16 inches","17 inches","18 inches"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. As a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, the neck circumference threshold is 17 inches or more in men and 16 inches in women, and the same cut-offs appear on physical examination. The screening questionnaire is different: it scores 16 inches for anyone. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 17"},{"q":"During an obstructive apnea, what happens to respiratory effort?","choices":["It stops along with airflow","It stops, then later resumes","It alternates with hyperventilation","It continues without airflow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In an obstructive apnea there is no airflow, but respiratory effort continues: the upper airway collapses while the patient still tries to breathe. That persisting effort is what separates it from a central apnea, where effort is absent too. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 24"},{"q":"Many patients with obesity hypoventilation syndrome present late with which end-stage picture?","choices":["Hypoxemic hypercapnic respiratory failure","Hyperventilation alternating with apnea","Isolated left-sided heart failure","Low carbon dioxide with normal oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Many patients present late, with end-stage features: severe hypoxemic hypercapnic respiratory failure and right-sided heart failure from pulmonary hypertension. Earlier symptoms are not specific and reflect obesity and coexisting obstructive apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 30, 31, 32"},{"q":"Without a qualifying symptom, how many predominantly obstructive respiratory events per hour of sleep confirm obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["5 or more","10 or more","15 or more","20 or more"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fifteen or more predominantly obstructive events per hour confirm obstructive sleep apnea on their own, provided the findings are not explained by another condition, medication or substance. With 5 or more events, a qualifying symptom is also required. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which condition belongs in the differential diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Nocturnal seizures","Enlarged tonsils","Poorly controlled hypertension","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nocturnal seizures are in the differential of obstructive sleep apnea, with central sleep apnea, nocturnal dyspnea from heart failure, swallowing disorders, panic attacks, nocturnal asthma, reflux and other causes of daytime sleepiness such as narcolepsy. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 10, 18, 20"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Reduced inspiratory muscle strength","Fat carried at the hips, not the waist","Mild obstructive sleep apnea","Preserved lung function"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors include reduced inspiratory muscle strength, central obesity (a high waist-to-hip ratio), reduced lung function due to obesity and severe obstructive sleep apnea. The syndrome becomes more likely as body mass index increases. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 29, 30"},{"q":"What adjustment applies when an elderly patient with obstructive sleep apnea is given modafinil or armodafinil?","choices":["Higher doses","No adjustment by age","A sedative instead","Lower doses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Modafinil and armodafinil are given at lower doses in the elderly population. The stimulants also carry many potential drug interactions and side effects such as headaches, nervousness or anxiety, dizziness and nausea, and need caution in cardiovascular, hepatic and psychiatric conditions. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 11, 15"},{"q":"In which setting has the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire been used?","choices":["Confirming the diagnosis","Grading disease severity","Preoperative evaluation","Community-wide screening"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The questionnaire has been used in preoperative evaluation to find people without a known diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea who may be at risk for perioperative complications. It has the highest sensitivity among the questionnaires. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 16"},{"q":"Which risk modification is advised for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Stop nasal steroids for rhinitis","Avoid alcohol and other sedatives","Sleep supine to open the airway","Take a sedative to deepen sleep"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Risk modification for obstructive sleep apnea means avoiding alcohol and other sedatives, together with diet and exercise for weight loss. Alcohol and sedative use is itself a risk factor for the disorder. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 11, 12, 15"},{"q":"A woman answers yes to loud snoring and to being observed to stop breathing during sleep; her body mass index is 38 kg/m2 and her shirt collar is 16 inches. She is not tired during the day, is not treated for high blood pressure and is not older than 50. What is her STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) total?","choices":["3","4","5","6"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + observed apnea 1 + body mass index above 35 1 + neck 16 inches or larger 1 = 4; tiredness, pressure, age and gender (female) score 0. A total of 4 is intermediate, but yes to 2 of the first four questions plus a body mass index above 35 makes her high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What causes the breathing events of obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Upper airway narrowing","Loss of brainstem drive","Excess sensitivity to carbon dioxide","Weak inspiratory muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive sleep apnea occurs because the upper airway narrows, from excessive soft tissue or structural abnormalities such as macroglossia, retrognathia or enlarged tonsils. The airway collapses while the patient still tries to breathe. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 24, 29"},{"q":"Which surgical procedure is a cure for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Palatal implants","Radiofrequency ablation","Bariatric surgery","Tracheostomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tracheostomy is a cure for obstructive sleep apnea: it bypasses the site of upper airway obstruction, so air no longer passes the collapsing segment. Other surgery, such as radiofrequency ablation or palatal implants, targets milder disease in patients who cannot tolerate pressure therapy. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 13, 33"},{"q":"Why might an at-home sleep test miss obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Moderate disease can be missed","Severe disease can be missed","It does not test for obstruction","Mild disease can be missed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At-home kits will not always diagnose mild obstructive sleep apnea; they are most helpful for moderate to severe disease. They also assess only obstructive apnea, not other sleep disorders, so a polysomnogram remains the gold standard. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 26"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for central sleep apnea?","choices":["Retrognathia","Macroglossia","Opioid medications","Enlarged tonsils"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Opioid medications are a risk factor for central sleep apnea, with heart failure, stroke, hypoxia, high altitude and use of continuous positive airway pressure. When opioids are the cause, the first management step is to reduce them. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 24, 26"},{"q":"What body mass index meets the obesity criterion for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Above 40 kg/m2","Above 30 kg/m2","Above 25 kg/m2","Above 35 kg/m2"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obesity for this diagnosis means a body mass index above 30, together with awake arterial carbon dioxide above 45 mmHg and exclusion of other causes. The syndrome becomes more likely as body mass index increases, and patients are typically obese and hypersomnolent. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 17, 28, 30, 32"},{"q":"Which polysomnogram finding confirms central sleep apnea?","choices":["Effort that resumes late in each event","Raised carbon dioxide on nocturnal monitoring","Apneas without thoracic-abdominal excursion","Apneas with continued respiratory effort"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea is diagnosed by polysomnography showing repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion: no airflow and no respiratory effort. An at-home test is not sufficient, since home kits assess only obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 8, 26, 32"},{"q":"Which treatment for obstructive sleep apnea is not effective in severe disease or in a patient with no teeth?","choices":["Oral appliance","Positional therapy","Tracheostomy","Continuous positive airway pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An oral appliance is not effective in severe obstructive sleep apnea, nor in patients who have no teeth. Those patients need another option, such as continuous positive airway pressure, the most effective therapy. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"Which second-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome is very effective?","choices":["Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Bariatric surgery for weight loss","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Autotitrating positive airway pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For patients who fail or do not tolerate first-line therapy, tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation is very effective. Bariatric surgery may be helpful, and medications are reserved for those who fail diet and exercise measures. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 26, 33"},{"q":"Which test is the gold standard for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Polysomnography with nocturnal carbon dioxide","At-home sleep apnea testing kit","Split-night study with pressure titration","Pulmonary function tests and chest radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polysomnography with continuous nocturnal carbon dioxide monitoring is the gold standard for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Serum bicarbonate and an arterial blood gas guide the workup before it, and other causes of hypoventilation must be excluded. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 32"},{"q":"What is first-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Weight-loss medications from the start","Weight loss plus positive airway pressure","Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Bariatric surgery as initial therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy is a weight loss program plus positive airway pressure, continuous or bilevel, within a multidisciplinary approach that adds lifestyle modification: diet changes, exercise and behavioral modifications. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In which patient with central sleep apnea is adaptive servo-ventilation especially avoided?","choices":["Central apnea that began at high altitude","Central apnea that persists after a stroke","Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","Central apnea from long-term opioid use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adaptive servo-ventilation is not used for all patients, especially those with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: with poor left ventricular function, reducing preload can decrease cardiac output. Cheyne-Stokes breathing may also be protective in such patients. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 26"},{"q":"Untreated obstructive sleep apnea triples the risk of which condition?","choices":["Hypertension","Driving accidents","Insulin resistance","Gout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries 3 times the risk of hypertension, and poorly controlled hypertension is itself a clue to the disorder. Blood pressure also becomes more variable, and sympathetic tone rises. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 18, 20, 21"},{"q":"When are nasal steroids used in obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["For nighttime reflux from the apnea","For congestion from allergic rhinitis","For persistent daytime sleepiness","For events that occur when supine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nasal steroids are used in obstructive sleep apnea for nasal congestion and inflammation caused by allergic rhinitis. They treat that nasal problem; the most effective therapy for the apnea itself remains continuous positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 12, 19"},{"q":"On the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, a man answers yes to loud snoring and daytime tiredness and no to every other question except gender. Which risk group applies?","choices":["Low: two symptom answers are too few","No group: no apnea was observed","High: two of the first four plus male","Intermediate: a total of 3 yes answers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tiredness 1 + male gender 1 = 3, which by total alone would be intermediate. But yes to 2 or more of the first four questions plus male gender is a high-risk combination, so he is high risk despite a total below 5. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which examination finding is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea rather than a screening feature for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Hepatomegaly","Enlarged tonsils","Facial plethora","Pedal edema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enlarged tonsils are a risk factor and examination finding of obstructive sleep apnea, alongside obesity, macroglossia, micrognathia, retrognathia, a thick neck and a crowded posterior oropharynx. They point to structural narrowing of the upper airway. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 31"},{"q":"Which symptoms persist in elderly patients with obstructive sleep apnea and improve with continuous positive airway pressure?","choices":["Insomnia and nocturnal dyspnea","Pedal edema and hepatomegaly","Exertional dyspnea and plethora","Drowsiness and nocturia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mortality from obstructive sleep apnea is lower in the elderly because they compensate, yet drowsiness and nocturia persist, and both improve with continuous positive airway pressure. Nocturia is also a core history finding of the disorder at any age. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 22, 25, 31"},{"q":"Which test is the gold standard for diagnosing obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["At-home testing kit","Screening questionnaire","Arterial blood gas","Polysomnogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A polysomnogram is the gold standard, whether run as a split-night study (2 hours of baseline, then pressure titration) or recorded through the whole night. It yields the apnea-hypopnea and respiratory disturbance indices used to confirm the diagnosis. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 16, 32"},{"q":"Which level falls in untreated obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Amyloid","Growth hormone","Cortisol","C-reactive protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Among the endocrine effects of untreated obstructive sleep apnea, growth hormone and prolactin decrease, while C-reactive protein, cortisol and amyloid increase. Proinflammatory cytokines rise as well and improve with continuous positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 21, 22"}]};
